INT. DRAGON'S LIBRARY, MOUNTAIN KEEP - DUSK

Shelves climb into darkness. A fire the size of a cart burns in a hearth carved like a mouth. Scrolls curl like sleeping snakes.

QUEEN ISOLDE VANTERPOOL (50s, gray-braided, scholar's robe) sits at a reading desk. Beside her, her scribe THADDEUS WINNOWMERE (30s, ink-stained fingers, glasses on a cord) copies lines from a vellum sheet. Her daughter, PRINCESS ASTRID VANTERPOOL (20, sword at her hip, boots caked in mud), strides in from the stairs, and behind her limps OLD MARROW (70s, one-eyed, a bundle of dried herbs), the keep's gardener.

ASTRID
Mother. I've come from the northern gate.

ISOLDE
So I see.

She does not look up. Astrid lays a muddy glove on the desk. Thaddeus shifts his ink pot an inch to the left.

ASTRID
They say the dragon has not slept in nine years.

ISOLDE
Nine years, four months, and three days.

ASTRID
You've been counting.

ISOLDE
Somebody must.

Marrow sets the herbs on the hearthstone and stirs them with a finger. The fire hisses. Astrid's jaw works.

ASTRID
I thought you were going to speak with him today.

ISOLDE
I did.

ASTRID
And?

ISOLDE
He asked me to read to him.

ASTRID
Read what?

ISOLDE
The ledgers.

Astrid blinks. Thaddeus coughs behind his hand. Marrow, at the fire, begins to hum.

ASTRID
Mother, he has burned villages.

ISOLDE
He has also asked after your sister every day for nine years.

ASTRID
I don't have a sister.

ISOLDE
(finally looking up)
No. You don't.

The hum stops. Marrow turns his one good eye on Isolde, then on Astrid, then, slowly, toward the fire. Thaddeus lays down his pen. The flames shrink a shade, as though listening.

ASTRID
What does that mean?

ISOLDE
It means sit down, child. And bring the ledger. It's a long story, and the dragon says you ought to hear it from me.
